  "text": "In this work we build explicit formulas for the mathematical objects that appear in the general quantum description of systems whose states can be localized in space, and we directly calculate how these objects fail to commute with each other. We then identify the circumstances under which the average values of physical quantities, or of their spatial densities, remain unchanged when the surface used to define them is described in a different way. It is demonstrated that, among the two paired field variables in an alternative formulation, one behaves as a quantity that does not depend on the chosen surface description, while its partner acts as a density that is likewise independent of that description. Finally, we show that the higher‑order momentum‑type operator in the original framework cannot be expressed more simply than in the alternative approach, confirming that, for practical calculations, the two formulations are effectively the same."
